  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Spotty light showers/drizzle tonight but much \nof the time it will be dry. More humid Sun with a few showers & \nt-storms possible especially near & west of the CT River Valley \nwhere an isolated strong-severe t-storm or two is possible.\n\nWeak offshore low pressure will continue to result in moist/light NE \nlow level flow into tonight. The result will be rather cloudy skies \ntonight with just some spotty light showers/drizzle at times...but \nthis activity will be quite light with much of the time featuring \ndry weather. Some shortwave energy may allow for a few elevated \nheavier showers and perhaps a couple t-storms to develop near the \nsoutheast New England coast toward daybreak...mainly near the Cape \nand Islands. Some of the earlier model guidance has this activity \nfurther west...but latest indicating that the main risk for this \nwill be towards the Cape and Islands closer to the shortwave energy.\n\nOvernight low temps will only drop into the lower to middle 60s \ngiven clouds and an abundance of low level moisture. Areas of fog \nwill be possible too...especially near the coast and in the \nWorcester Hills.\n\nAgain...we will need to watch for a few showers and isolated t-\nstorms near the Cape and Islands early Sun morning. Otherwise...the \nmain concern for a few showers and thunderstorms will be Sun \nafternoon/early evening given diurnal instability and shortwave \nenergy to our west. The bulk of the height falls/forcing  will be to \nour west. Therefore...greatest risk for a few showers and \nthunderstorms will be near and west of the CT River Valley Sun \nafternoon/early evening. Instability will be limited by a lot of \ncloud cover...but effective shear of 40+ knots will result in the \nlow risk for an isolated strong to severe severe thunderstorm or \ntwo. Main severe weather risk would be a very localized damaging \nwind gusts and brief torrential rainfall. Greatest risk for this \nwould near and west of the CT River Valley. That being said...much \nof the day will feature dry weather...but with a bit more humidity \ncompared to this afternoon. Highs will be in the 70s to near 80.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier with a return to seasonable temperatures \nnext week. Rain chances return Thursday/Friday.\n\nA post-frontal air mass will usher in drier conditions and warmer \ntemperatures with westerly winds Monday. As skies gradually clear \nfrom the dreary weekend, dewpoints will begin to drop slightly... \ninto the upper 50s as opposed to the low to mid 60s we've been \nseeing. High pressure begins to slowly build Tuesday into Wednesday, \nprolonging the clear skies, drier weather, and warmer temperatures \nthrough the middle of the week. Chances for rain showers return \nThursday and Friday as a low pressure system develops over the Great \nLakes and meanders east through southern Ontario. Some uncertainty \nregarding the onset of the showers as there's little consistency in \nthe global ensembles on the actual placement of the low as it tracks \nthrough Canada.",
